[11.145548, "o", "\u001b[32m╭─\u001b[0m\u001b[32m──────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[0m\u001b[32m \u001b[0m\u001b[1;32mallam-2-7b\u001b[0m\u001b[32m \u001b[0m\u001b[32m──────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[0m\u001b[32m─╮\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[32m│\u001b[0m                                                                                                  \u001b[32m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[32m│\u001b[0m  Python decorators are a powerful tool that allow you to modify the behavior of functions,       \u001b[32m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[32m│\u001b[0m  classes, and methods without directly modifying their code. They are defined using the '@'      \u001b[32m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[32m│\u001b[0m  symbol followed by the decorator function name. When a decorated function, class, or method is  \u001b[32m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[32m│\u001b[0m  called, the decorator is executed before the original code, enabling you to add new             \u001b[32m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[32m│\u001b[0m  functionality or modify existing ones. Decorators can be used for tasks like logging, caching,  \u001b[32m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[32m│\u001b[0m  parameter validation, or even creating profiling tools. They provide a clean and concise way    \u001b[32m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[32m│\u001b[0m  to apply complex modifications to your code, making your programs more maintainable and easier  \u001b[32m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[32m│\u001b[0m  to read.                                                                                        \u001b[32m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[32m│\u001b[0m                                                                                                  \u001b[32m│\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[32m╰─\u001b[0m\u001b[32m────────────────────────────\u001b[0m\u001b[32m \u001b[0m\u001b[2;32m42 prompt + 131 completion = 173 total\u001b[0m\u001b[32m \u001b[0m\u001b[32m────────────────────────────\u001b[0m\u001b[32m─╯\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[13.510466, "o", "\u001b[H\u001b[2J\u001b[3J"]
